+#### Pruning merged branches and worktrees
+
+Finished branches and their worktrees are pruned without you asking. Preview it any time:
+
+```bash
+ta cleanup --only branches --dry-run   # each local branch, the evidence, and what would happen
+ta cleanup --only branches             # delete the safe ones, ask about the rest
+```
+
+The pull request state comes from your VCS adapter, not from `git branch -d` or `git cherry`, because a squash merge rewrites commits and makes both call a finished branch unmerged. A local branch is deleted without asking only when one of these is true:
+
+- Its pull request is merged and the branch has no commits beyond the PR head.
+- Its tip is already contained in the default branch (and is not simply the default branch's own tip, which could be a branch created a moment ago).
+
+Everything else is a question with "keep" recommended: an open PR, no PR, a PR closed without merging, a merged PR with commits added afterwards, or a PR state that could not be checked. The current branch, the default branch and any branch checked out in the main worktree are never touched. A branch checked out in a linked worktree is pruned together with that worktree, but only when the worktree has no modified or untracked files. If it does, you get a question listing the files and nothing is removed. While a `ta draft apply` is running, branches are left alone.
+
+Every deletion is written to `.ta/cleanup/branches.jsonl` and `.ta/audit.jsonl` with the branch name and its full tip SHA, so it can be brought back:
+
+```bash
+git branch <name> <sha>      # the exact command is in the log line
+```
+
+Remote branches are never deleted (let the host delete merged branches: GitHub's "Automatically delete head branches"). Remote branches whose PR was closed without merging are listed as findings so you can decide.
+
+This runs on its own: right after `ta draft watch` (or `ta draft apply --watch`) sees the PR merge, and in the daemon's periodic cleanup, even when `auto = "off"`. Turn it off with `prune_branches = false`; naming it explicitly with `--only branches` still works.
+
+prune_branches = true # Prune finished local branches and clean worktrees (see "Pruning merged branches and worktrees")
